In article <393@lily.warwick.ac.uk> cstxqhh@warwick.ac.uk (RouE)
brings up the question of the relative technical difficulty of
multi-player-ising NetHack as against the Manifest and Manifold
Amazing Benefits thereof. Well, it's true, but it is unlikely to
happen IMHO. But I'd like to comment on WHY.

				~ * ~

There are a number of things that it would be fairly transparently
'nice' to add to NetHack: multiplayer ability and levels that adapt to
the size of the window are clearly among them.

There are three distinct reasons why such things might be headaches:-

The first is technical implementability (if you look at how many
global variables there are in NH, for example, you will realise that
making it go multiplayer would be more than a small task).

The second is game mechanics. If you contemplate the time model and
the way compute load is distributed over simulated time in NetHack
(which is to say, we do all the work when it is needed, no matter how
long, rt, this makes the player wait), you will realise that,
technical difficulties or not, NetHack *cannot* be multiplayer and
still maintain its character - becausae it is the *nature* of NetHack
that all the monsters WAIT for you to take your turn, and then YOU
wait for THEM. If some of them are other player characters, then
EITHER the game goes realtime, making an entirely DIFFERENT class and
flavour of game (and requiring great simplification of the rules (or
maybe a great complication in the architecture?), incidentally, to
keep the worst case global move computation down to a small fraction
of a second), OR everyone has to take their turn round-robin, which
would be pretty unplayable (you want to run down a corridor while she
wants to stare at her inventory and figure out what of this ghost's
stash to dump...).

The third is game balance. As recent discussions have marvellously
exemplified, players can't even agree on when the game is
well-balanced (is the fact that there are techniques that consistently
- say once in 50 games (to judge from the reroll statistics published
by one commentator) enable you to win an indicator of a
poorly-balanced game? I wouldn't have thought so, I would have said
that lends a certain, er, point to playing; but...). At any rate,
whatever balance there IS in the game is hard-won, and the result of
analysis by the developers of all the feedback they get from the
thousands of hours of game experience that gets reported here. Even so
'simple' a change as doubling the number of cells on a level
(expending the wondow size by a linear factor of only 1.4 :-) would
take that balance and throw it out the window - because it is a
learned balance, and it is beyond human knowing to have a model of
fair game play on which to base computed parameters (monster creation
rate or stronghold depth as a function of room count or level size,
for example).

Now, I'm not trying to discourage discussion; but I AM saying that the
code is probably grottier than you think (well, I'm down on it today
because I'm trying to make global changes and history and Messrs K&R
are consipring against me); and, far MORE importantly, there is a
great deal of EVOLVED GAME DESIGN in there, and even if something were
trivial to code it might throw a lot of things out of balance.

Ironically, if the unbalancing feature is too popular (can you say
'poly-piles'?), EVER recovering the balance may be beyond mortal
power.

				~ * ~

Oh, and I have my own 2 cents to contribute to the Poly Pile
Controversy: I think we could easily leave this (and many other
things!) in unchanged - IF WE REMOVED THE QUIT COMMAND.

If people have to go and FIND all the goodies they need to do this
kind of thing, well, more power to them. If they re-roll until they
get a starting inventory they like, well, let's make THAT take some
time and effort - and embarass them on the score screen (by putting a
'number of games not listed due to pathetic score' field in the high
score screen by each of the top ten players!)
